213: Let $\Lambda=\Lambda(x_1,x_2,\ldots)$ be the ring of symmetric functions with integral coefficients. This space has a ${\mathbb Z}$-linear basis consisting of \emph{Schur functions} $s_{\lambda}$,
214: where $\lambda$ is an integer partition. Consider the ideal
215: \[I_{r,n-r} = \mathrm{Span}_\mathbb{Z}\left\{s_\lambda\mid \lambda\nsubseteq (n-r)^r \right\} \]
216: of $\Lambda$. Then one has the following standard presentation of the cohomology of Grassmannians:
217: \[H^*(\mathrm{Gr}_{r,n})\cong \Lambda/I_{r,n-r}. \]
218: We will use an overline to denote the projection $\Lambda\to\Lambda/I_{r,n-r}$. Specifically, we will write $\overline{\Lambda}$ as a shorthand for $\Lambda/I_{r,n-r}$. We will also distinguish between $s_{\lambda}\in \Lambda$, the usual Schur function, and $\overline{s}_{\lambda}$ for its image in $\overline{\Lambda}$.
219: We resolve the minimality conjecture \cite[Conjecture~4.10]{RWY} about the basic ideals $J_v$:
220: \begin{theorem}\label{thm:main}
221: Given a bigrassmannian permutation $v=v_{r,s,t,n}\in S_n$ let
222: \begin{align*}
223: i&\coloneqq s-t+1,\\
224: j&\coloneqq r-t+1,\\
225: a&\coloneqq \min(n-r-i,r-j),\\
226: b&\coloneqq \min(i,j).
227: \end{align*}
228: Then the basic ideal $J_v$, thought of as an ideal of $H^*(\mathrm{Gr}_{r,n})\cong \Lambda/I_{r,n-r}$, is minimally generated by
229: \begin{equation}
230: \label{eqn:minimallist}
231: \{\overline{s}_{\mu} \mid i^j\subseteq \mu \subseteq (i^j,b^a)\}.
232: \end{equation}
233: \end{theorem}
234: That (\ref{eqn:minimallist}) generates $J_v$ is \cite[Theorem 4.8]{RWY}. The conjectural part, which we prove,
235: is the minimality claim, that is, no generator can be removed without changing the ideal.

294: Viewing the parameters $A_{\lambda,\theta}$ as indeterminates, a syzygy of the form (\ref{eqn:syzygy}) for some $n,r,i,j,a,b,N$ is equivalent to a solution of the simultaneous linear equations
295: \begin{equation}
296: \label{eqn:eqs}
297: \biggr\{ \sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} c_{(i^j,\lambda),\theta}^{\nu} \, A_{\lambda,\theta}=\chi(\nu)\,\biggr| \,\nu\subseteq (n-r)^r \biggr\},
298: \end{equation}
299: where $\chi(\nu)=f_\rho$ when $\nu=(i^j,\rho)$ for some $\rho\subseteq b^a$ with $|\rho|=N$, and $\chi(\nu)=0$ otherwise.
300: The following definition records the assumptions on the parameters used in the preceding considerations.
301: \begin{definition}
302: Call an integer tuple $\varphi=(n,r,i,j,a,b,N)$ \emph{valid} if $n,r,i,j,a,b,N\geq 1$ with
303: \[r<n,\quad a+j\leq r,\quad a+i\leq n-r,\quad b\leq i,j,\quad\mbox{and}\quad N\leq ab. \]
304: \end{definition}
305: Due to vanishing of Littlewood--Richardson coefficients, not all partitions $\nu\subseteq (n-r)^r$ contribute meaningfully to (\ref{eqn:eqs}).
306: \begin{definition}
307: Call a partition $\nu$ with $i^j\subseteq\nu\subseteq (n-r)^r$ and $|\nu|=ij+N$ \emph{allowable}.
308: \end{definition}
309: When $\nu$ is not allowable, both $\chi(\nu)$ and all the Littlewood--Richardson coefficients $c_{(i^j,\lambda),\theta}^{\nu}$ vanish. In this case both sides of the corresponding equation in (\ref{eqn:eqs}) are zero, so we can assume that $\nu$ is always allowable in~(\ref{eqn:eqs}). We will actually show that the equations (\ref{eqn:eqs}) are inconsistent by focusing on a smaller subset of them.
310: \begin{definition}
311: Call an allowable partition $\nu$ \emph{decomposable} if $\nu_{j+1}\leq b$ (taking $\nu_{j+1}=0$ if $\ell(\nu)= j$), and $\ell(\nu)\leq j+a$. Write $\decomp_{\varphi}$ for the set of decomposable partitions $\nu$. We will decompose any $\nu\in\decomp_{\varphi}$ into 3 smaller partitions via Figure \ref{fig:decomposition}.
312: \end{definition}
313: We will similarly say that an equation in (\ref{eqn:eqs}) is \emph{decomposable} if the partition $\nu$ it corresponds to is decomposable.
